                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_both:          `<strong>That’s the mandate honoured.</strong> Running both lenses before you price is exactly what the board ordered after the last deal. ESG and trust are value-protection, not delay — and you’ll price the bid on what the financials actually hide.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diligence.</strong> Both lenses run, the €45M remediation and transition risk quantified, the founder/customer dependency sized. You now know what the clean financials don’t show — and you can price and structure for it instead of being blindsided post-close.`,
                    coach_r1_esgonly:       `<strong>Half the mandate.</strong> You’ve covered permits and carbon — but the founder owns the top customer relationships and the workforce is wary. That trust exposure is where 20–30% of your synergy lives. Skipping it is how the last deal lost the relationships it paid for.`,
                    coach_r1_speed:         `<strong>Fast and blind — the exact failure mode the board forbade.</strong> You signed on clean financials before the rival moved. The reps won’t cover the permit gap because you never asked, and the founder’s retention terms are silent. Speed beat the rival and repeated the last deal’s mistake.`,
                    coach_r1_esgdismiss:    `<strong>“Just paperwork” is how €45M becomes a post-close surprise.</strong> Two sites lack EU-aligned permits and carbon is +40% — that is a value-relevant finding, not a formality. Quantify it now, or your group carbon intensity and a remediation order hit you eighteen months in.`,
                    coach_r1_esgwalk:       `<strong>Walking over the carbon gap throws out a value-creating platform.</strong> A +40% intensity gap and a €45M remediation are <em>priceable</em>, not fatal. ESG is a valuation input — abandoning the deal forfeits the CEE entry the board mandated.`,
                    coach_r1_trustignore:   `<strong>“The workforce will adapt” underrates founder dependency.</strong> The founder personally owns the top customer relationships; cultural distance and low trust put 20–30% of synergy at risk. Treat it as standard and you’ll lose the relationships you paid the premium for.`,
                    coach_r1_trustwalk:     `<strong>“Founder-owned can never integrate” is over-reading the risk.</strong> Founder dependency is a <em>structurable</em> exposure — earn-outs and lock-ins manage it. Assuming zero synergy walks away from a platform that the right structure makes work.`,

                    coach_r2_esgfull:       `<strong>ESG priced as cash flows — that’s the lesson learned.</strong> Remediation capex, transition risk and the cost-of-capital effect all in the price. The €45M is no longer a post-close surprise; it’s a number the IC can see and the seller can’t spring on you later.`,
                    coach_r2_esgpartial:    `<strong>You took the €45M off enterprise value — a start.</strong> But transition risk and the green cost-of-capital effect are still unpriced. The single largest ESG adjustment isn’t just the capex; it’s how the gap moves your financing cost and your group profile.`,
                    coach_r2_esgnone:       `<strong>Financials-only pricing — exactly what sank the last deal.</strong> The €45M remediation and the transition risk aren’t post-close operating noise; they’re value. Price on clean numbers and the IC will ask, eighteen months on, where the €45M showed up in your bid. It didn’t.`,
                    coach_r2_synfull:       `<strong>The trust haircut is in the case.</strong> Pricing the 25% synergy loss from founder/customer dependency means the value you present is the value you can realise — not a number that evaporates when the founder leaves with the customers.`,
                    coach_r2_synnone:       `<strong>No haircut means you’re modelling synergies you can’t keep.</strong> The founder owns the relationships; cultural distance is real. A deal case with full synergies and an unmanaged founder is a number that looks great until the customers follow him out the door.`,
                    coach_r2_crosswalk:     `<strong>Your bid is above your own walk-away.</strong> A walk-away you cross isn’t discipline — it’s decoration. The premium over comps has to be backed by synergy you can realise; past that line you’re destroying value to win. What was the walk-away for?`,
                    coach_r2_overpay:       `<strong>Bidding at the owners’ 8.5x ask is the winner’s curse waiting to happen.</strong> €510M is well above the 6.5–7.0x comps, and the premium isn’t backed by synergy. You may get the asset — but winning a deal and creating value are not the same thing.`,
                    coach_r2_walkhigh:      `<strong>A walk-away near the owners’ ask is no discipline at all.</strong> If you’ll pay almost anything, you have no walk-away. Set it where comps plus realisable synergy actually support — that’s the line that protects you when the rival escalates.`,
                    coach_r2_underbid:      `<strong>Below the 6.5x comp floor, you lose the platform.</strong> Under-bidding hands the asset to the rival. Discipline isn’t the lowest number — it’s the highest number still backed by synergy, at or below your walk-away.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>Bid committed.</strong> Now the test: is the premium over comps backed by synergy, is it at or below your walk-away, and will the structure keep leverage under 2.5x? Price is only half the deal.`,

                    coach_r3_breach:        `<strong>You’ve breached the 2.5x ceiling.</strong> An all-cash bid at your multiple puts post-close leverage through the board’s line. Treasury says the equity component you avoided — and the sustainability-linked facility you skipped — would have kept you under it. The structure has to win the asset <em>and</em> respect the discipline.`,
                    coach_r3_overdilute:    `<strong>Heavy equity keeps you under the ceiling, but it dilutes.</strong> Paper has a price too. Use just enough equity/paper to respect the 2.5x line — over-diluting gives away value you fought to create in the price.`,
                    coach_r3_earnboth:      `<strong>An earn-out on both milestones bridges the gap and transfers risk.</strong> Tying deferred consideration to ESG compliance and customer retention means the seller carries the exposure you diligenced — and you pay full value only if the synergies actually arrive.`,
                    coach_r3_lockfull:      `<strong>Lock-in plus enhanced reps protects what you paid for.</strong> The founder’s relationships stay, the permit gap is contractually covered. This is trust risk turned into a structured, priced, protected exposure — exactly what the last deal lacked.`,
                    coach_r3_locknone:      `<strong>All-cash, clean exit — and there go the relationships.</strong> The founder takes the cheque, and within a year the customers he personally owned have followed him out the door. You bought the company but not the relationships. An earn-out or lock-in would have protected the value you paid for.`,
                    coach_r3_slf:           `<strong>The sustainability-linked facility earns its place.</strong> A ≈35 bps margin ratchet tied to the target’s decarbonization lowers your financing cost and aligns incentives — and it eases the ratio at the margin. Sustainable finance as a structuring lever, not a press release.`,
                    coach_r3_noslf:         `<strong>You skipped the sustainability-linked facility.</strong> A ≈35 bps ratchet tied to decarbonization would have shaved financing cost and aligned the founder’s incentives with your net-zero pathway. On an ESG-gapped target, that’s a lever you left on the table.`,

                    coach_r4_hold:          `<strong>That’s the discipline.</strong> You held your bid and stood ready to walk above your walk-away. Winning a deal and creating value are not the same thing — and the IC trusts a leader who can name the price they won’t pay, even with a rival in the room.`,
                    coach_r4_capitulate:    `<strong>Matching at 8.7x crosses any disciplined walk-away.</strong> You met the market and the market was over your line. You’ll likely get the asset — but the premium isn’t backed by synergy, and the IC will ask what value you created by winning.`,
                    coach_r4_raise:         `<strong>Raising to 8.9x is the winner’s curse, textbook.</strong> €534M, well above comps, the premium unbacked by synergy, and the extra cash pushes leverage back through the ceiling. You got the asset and destroyed value doing it. What was your walk-away — and why did you cross it?`,
                    coach_r4_integrated:    `<strong>That’s the case the IC approves.</strong> Price, ESG and trust adjustments, structure, financing cost and completion probability — defended as one. You held the walk-away, the value is real and realisable, and the structure pre-empts the two failure modes that sank the last deal.`,
                    coach_r4_strategic:     `<strong>Strategic fit alone won’t carry the IC.</strong> The upside story is fine, but the committee remembers the last deal. Without the ESG and trust adjustments and the completion math next to it, “great platform” reads as the same optimism that misfired before.`,
                    coach_r4_priceonly:     `<strong>“We got it below the ask” isn’t value creation.</strong> A low headline price with unpriced ESG and an unmanaged founder is the cheap deal that gets expensive post-close. Price is one input — the IC wants the integrated case.`,
                    coach_r4_deny:          `<strong>“Nothing to pre-empt” is how the post-mortem writes itself.</strong> The last deal’s post-mortem blamed unpriced ESG and lost trust. If your structure doesn’t name and cover those, you’re trusting a model — and the model didn’t save the last team.`,

